    "body": "#### vim-patch:9.2.1100: NFA regexp engine does not inline copy_sub()\n\nProblem:  copy_sub() is on the hottest path of the NFA regexp engine, since\n          addstate() calls it for every state it adds, but at -O2 the\n          compiler leaves it out of line so every call pays function-call\n          overhead.\nSolution: Mark copy_sub() \"static inline\" so the compiler folds it into its\n          callers, which slightly increases binary size.\n\nMeasured over a buffer of C code, regexp search and substitute are\nabout 6-8% faster, including patterns with capturing groups.\n\ncloses: vim/vim#21300\n\nhttps://github.com/vim/vim/commit/3152d64a2b4848b578bd4c243e8d0f3d52ae6b5e\n\nCo-authored-by: Julien Voisin <julien.voisin@dustri.org>\n\n\n#### vim-patch:9.2.1101: modifier_len() scans the whole command modifier table\n\nProblem:  modifier_len() always iterates every entry of cmdmod_info_tab[]\n          when checking for a command modifier, even though the table is\n          sorted by name; this runs for every sourced line via\n          has_loop_cmd() and shows up when loading large syntax scripts.\nSolution: Break out of the loop as soon as the table entry's first letter\n          is past the command's first letter, since no later entry can\n          match in a name-sorted table (Julien Voisin).\n\nAdding an early break roughly halves the instruction count spent in\nmodifier_len() and shaves about 1.5% to 2% off single-file syntax load across\ncommon filetypes, with no change in behaviour.\n\ncloses: vim/vim#21304\n\nhttps://github.com/vim/vim/commit/4600f4afec028c049d254a89929e762f6442d549\n\nCo-authored-by: Julien Voisin <julien.voisin@dustri.org>\n\n\n#### vim-patch:9.2.1103: regexp compilation decodes ASCII bytes as multi-byte\n\nProblem:  When compiling a regexp the lexer calls mb_ptr2char() and\n          utf_ptr2len() for every character, including plain ASCII, even\n          though a byte below 0x80 is always a single character equal to\n          itself. Patterns are mostly ASCII, so this is wasted work on the\n          hot compile path.\nSolution: In peekchr() and skipchr() take a fast path for bytes below 0x80,\n          skipping the multi-byte decode calls (Julien Voisin).\n\npeekchr() already holds regparse[0] in \"curchr\", so a byte below 0x80 needs no\nmb_ptr2char() call; the backslashed case is handled the same way. skipchr()\nlikewise advances by one byte directly instead of calling utf_ptr2len(). Both\nregexp engines share this lexer, so both compile faster.\n\nNFA pattern compilation is about 5% cheaper (skipchr() -29%, peekchr() -13%);\nloading a set of syntax files is about 1.4% faster end to end.\n\ncloses: vim/vim#21301\n\nhttps://github.com/vim/vim/commit/e5da1f1f037d2549c883db709ecde2928d0f047e\n\nCo-authored-by: Julien Voisin <julien.voisin@dustri.org>",
